      "dom_id": "question-71397",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$",
